```

### Key Components

#### SSE Client (`sse-client.ts`)

- Creates a browser SSE connection to `/api/investigate`
- Maps process SSE events plus a separate report replay SSE endpoint
- Receives `investigation_id` early, then reconnects to `/api/investigate/report` after the process flow completes
- Auto-closes on `complete` event
- Emits terminal `{ recoverable: false }` callback when the transport drops before completion
- Returns `{ close(): void }` handle for cleanup

#### Narrative Page (`routes/+page.svelte`)

- Maintains `narrativeLines`, `detectiveText`, `completion`, `error`, and scroll anchor state with Svelte 5 runes
- Converts `phase_start`, `source_status`, `progress`, `summary_result`, and `extraction_result` events into a single ordered feed
- Keeps the detective markdown separate from the process narrative so the final report can be replayed after completion or reconnect
- Preserves narrative continuity on recoverable errors by appending warning copy and waiting for fallback `detective_stream` replacement
- Auto-scrolls while the user stays near the bottom; stops forcing scroll once the user manually scrolls up

#### Query Type Auto-Detection

- `search-box.svelte` infers type from regex heuristics: leading `0...` phone numbers, numeric bank account candidates, and URL-like input
- Users can override the detected type explicitly; `manualOverride` prevents hydration-time oscillation between inferred and chosen values
- `+page.ts` validates `type` from URL params and falls back to `phone`
- Shareable URLs such as `/?q=0926408013&type=phone` auto-start the same narrative investigation flow

#### Markdown Rendering

```typescript
// src/lib/markdown.ts
function sanitizeMarkdown(md: string): string
```

- Uses `marked` (GFM + breaks enabled) for markdown-to-HTML conversion
- Custom renderer: links open in new tab with `rel="noopener noreferrer"`, `javascript:` URLs blocked
- DOMPurify sanitization with `target`, `rel`, `class` attributes allowed
- `narrative-conclusion.svelte` strips the machine-readable footer lines before rendering
- Prevents XSS in detective report output

#### Evidence and Risk Presentation

- `evidence-card.svelte` renders both source summaries and deep URL extractions in one expandable card component
- `risk-badge.svelte` normalizes `risk_level` text defensively and clamps `confidence` to `0..100%` for display
- `narrative-stream-copy.ts` converts raw phase/source events into Vietnamese narration tuned to the detective flow
- `app.css` adds narrative-specific motion and conclusion styling (`narrative-fade-in`, `narrative-conclusion`)

#### Development Proxy and Production Routing

- `frontend/vite.config.ts` proxies `/api/*` to `http://localhost:3067` during local development
- No `frontend/src/routes/api/investigate/+server.ts` proxy exists in the current source tree
- Production deployment therefore needs same-origin API routing or an upstream reverse proxy that forwards `/api/investigate` to the Rust backend

#### Dark Mode

- System preference detection via `matchMedia('(prefers-color-scheme: dark)')`
- LocalStorage persistence under `theme` key
- Reactive Svelte 5 `$state` + `$effect` pattern
- Toggle button in `+layout.svelte` (top-right corner)

#### SEO Meta Tags

- Dynamic `<title>` based on current query
- Static `<meta name="description">` in Vietnamese
- Open Graph tags (`og:title`, `og:description`, `og:type`)
- `<meta name="robots" content="index, follow">`

#### Reactive State Pattern

All UI state managed via Svelte 5 runes:
- `$state<T>` for reactive narrative state, completion metadata, and view behavior
- `$derived` for normalized risk labels, detective HTML, busy state, and hydrated inputs
- State reset on new search, SSE callbacks append or replace data incrementally
- Cached investigations are replayed by the backend as normal SSE events, so the frontend renders live and cached sessions through the same narrative path

### Build & Deployment

| Command       | Description                    |
|---------------|-------------------------------|
| `npm run dev` | Vite dev server (port 5167)   |
| `npm run build` | Production build (`build/`) |
| `npm run preview` | Preview production build  |
| `npm run check` | TypeScript type checking    |

Production output uses `adapter-node` (Node.js server target).

### Development Proxy

During development, Vite proxies `/api` requests to `http://localhost:3067` (Rust backend), eliminating CORS issues.
